Done — it's already `#888` on disk (line 14; the earlier attempt landed, which is why the re-apply said "not found").

```css
#navigator-toolbox[zen-sidebar-expanded="true"] {
    border-left: 1px solid #888 !important;
}
```

The expanded sidebar's left border is now a clear medium grey instead of the near-white `#ccc`. **Restart Zen fully** to see it.

Note: the separate `#zen-sidebar-web-panel[pinned="true"]` rule (line 6) still uses `#ccc` — that's the border on a *pinned web panel*, a different element. If you want that one greyed to match too, say so and I'll change it. Otherwise this is the sidebar border you asked about.

Both files are already correct on disk — the pasted contents confirm it. Everything you asked for across this session is in place:

**`userContent.css`** — scrollbar bright thumb, transparent track (no hover-changing background):
```css
scrollbar-color: rgba(190, 190, 190, 0.95) transparent !important;
scrollbar-width: auto !important;
```

**`userChrome.css`** — sidebar left border now grey `#888` (was near-white `#ccc`), only while expanded:
```css
#navigator-toolbox[zen-sidebar-expanded="true"] {
    border-left: 1px solid #888 !important;
}
```

Nothing left to change. **Fully quit and reopen Zen** to load both files (they're only read at startup — closing the window isn't enough).

One thing still on `#ccc` that I left alone: the `#zen-sidebar-web-panel[pinned="true"]` border on line 6 — that's a *pinned web panel*, a different element from the sidebar. Say the word if you want that greyed to match too. Otherwise this is good to close out.

